    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test \u2014 the only test class in the project). \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented customer-code de-duplication so that distinct owners always receive distinct `customerCode`s.\n\n**The rule:** when the computed `<REGION>-<HASH8>` code already belongs to an existing owner, append `-<n>` with the smallest `n \u2265 2` that yields a free code.\n\n**Where the logic went:**\n\n- `CustomerCodeGenerator` (`service/`) \u2014 already the single unit responsible for \"producing the customer code assigned to a new owner,\" so it now also ensures uniqueness. It builds the base code via the existing `CustomerCode.of(...)` (unchanged, still owns the format), then a small private `deduplicate(...)` helper walks `-2`, `-3`, \u2026 until it finds an unclaimed code. It gained an `OwnerRepository` dependency; `ClinicServiceImpl` still just calls `generate(owner)` and is untouched.\n\n- `OwnerRepository` \u2014 added `boolean existsByCustomerCode(String)`, the collision check the generator needs, mirroring the existing `count*`/`exists`-style query methods.\n\n- Implemented that method in all three repository implementations that back the interface:\n  - `SpringDataOwnerRepository` (the active `spring-data-jpa` profile) \u2014 derived query, no `@Query` needed.\n  - `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl` (`jpa` profile) \u2014 JPQL `COUNT`.\n  - `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` (`jdbc` profile) \u2014 `COUNT(*)` on `customer_code`.\n\nThe `-<n>` suffix sits after the hash, so the region parser (`CustomerCode.regionOf`, which splits on the first `-`), the membership number, and the Luhn check digit all continue to work unchanged.",
